## 5. Limiting cases

| case | required behaviour | result |
|---|---|---|
| **S → 1 everywhere** (infinitely sensitive detector) | recover the current implementation exactly | `S̄_φ ≡ 1` ⇒ (T3′) → (T3); `S_4D ≡ 1` ⇒ `g_i^corr = g_i`. **PASS by construction** |
| **σ_cond → 0** (mass perfectly informative) | 2D factor → `S_4D` at the data; h-independent | (T1″); the implemented value is `8.8e-8`, i.e. already this limit. **PASS** |
| **σ_cond → ∞** (mass uninformative) | 2D channel must degenerate to the 1D channel | `N_cond → ` flat ⇒ `g_i^corr → ∫φ S_4D = S̄_φ` and (T1′) → (T3′). **PASS — and it exhibits the asymmetry as a continuous interpolation between the two limits** |
| **full data retained** (all 14 params observed) | no correction at all | `P(det|θ)` → indicator at the data = 1 for every detected event. **PASS** — the correction is exactly the price of reduced data |
| **data-thresholded detection** (SNR from the noisy data) | no correction in any channel | MFG standard form; C1 fails and the whole result is void. Consistent |
| **φ flat in `log₁₀M`, `S_4D` mass-independent** | 1D correction h-independent | `S̄_φ` = `S_3D(d_L(z;h))`, h-frozen at the peak ⇒ tilt → 0. Consistent with §3.2's attribution of the whole slope to the mass lift |

